    {
      "section_id": "section_Clinical_Description",
      "section_heading": "Clinical Description",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "paragraph_id": "p10",
          "paragraph_index": 10,
          "text": "Males with Y chromosome infertility usually have no symptoms other than infertility. A physical examination may reveal small testes in those with Sertoli cell-only (SCO) syndrome. Physical examination is normal in approximately 30% of males with Y chromosome infertility.",
          "local_clinical_domains": [],
          "section_id": "section_Clinical_Description",
          "section_heading": "Clinical Description",
          "sentences": [
            {
              "sentence_id": "p10_s1",
              "sentence_index": 1,
              "text": "Males with Y chromosome infertility usually have no symptoms other than infertility.",
              "char_start": 622,
              "char_end": 706
            },
            {
              "sentence_id": "p10_s2",
              "sentence_index": 2,
              "text": "A physical examination may reveal small testes in those with Sertoli cell-only (SCO) syndrome.",
              "char_start": 707,
              "char_end": 801
            },
            {
              "sentence_id": "p10_s3",
              "sentence_index": 3,
              "text": "Physical examination is normal in approximately 30% of males with Y chromosome infertility.",
              "char_start": 802,
              "char_end": 893
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "paragraph_id": "p11",
          "paragraph_index": 11,
          "text": "Males with Y chromosome infertility have azoospermia or severe, moderate, or mild oligozoospermia depending on the location and size of the Y chromosome deletion (see Genotype-Phenotype Correlations ). Most males with AZFa or AZFb/c deletions have a very poor prognosis for finding any sperm with testicular sperm extraction (TESE). Males with AZFc deletions (b2/b4 or gr/gr) have an extremely favorable prognosis (87%) for finding sperm sufficient for successful intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI).",
          "local_clinical_domains": [
            {
              "key": "renal_genitourinary",
              "label": "Renal / Genitourinary",
              "evidence_headings": [],
              "evidence_snippets": [
                "osis for finding any sperm with testicular sperm extraction (TESE). Males with AZFc deleti"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "section_id": "section_Clinical_Description",
          "section_heading": "Clinical Description",
          "sentences": [
            {
              "sentence_id": "p11_s1",
              "sentence_index": 1,
              "text": "Males with Y chromosome infertility have azoospermia or severe, moderate, or mild oligozoospermia depending on the location and size of the Y chromosome deletion (see Genotype-Phenotype Correlations ).",
              "char_start": 895,
              "char_end": 1096
            },
            {
              "sentence_id": "p11_s2",
              "sentence_index": 2,
              "text": "Most males with AZFa or AZFb/c deletions have a very poor prognosis for finding any sperm with testicular sperm extraction (TESE).",
              "char_start": 1097,
              "char_end": 1227
            },
            {
              "sentence_id": "p11_s3",
              "sentence_index": 3,
              "text": "Males with AZFc deletions (b2/b4 or gr/gr) have an extremely favorable prognosis (87%) for finding sperm sufficient for successful intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI).",
              "char_start": 1228,
              "char_end": 1399
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "paragraph_id": "p12",
          "paragraph_index": 12,
          "text": "Oligozoospermia may be compatible with fertility when the female partner is very fertile.",
          "local_clinical_domains": [
            {
              "key": "renal_genitourinary",
              "label": "Renal / Genitourinary",
